I was traveling, and that day happened to be the day Korea played in the World Cup.

251
00:23:30,410 --> 00:23:33,470
I just said it.

252
00:23:33,470 --> 00:23:41,220
Korean people eat chicken and beer, chimaek, when watching football.

253
00:23:41,220 --> 00:23:45,740
So I went to a chicken restaurant in Cambodia.

254
00:23:45,740 --> 00:23:52,380
Rather than a chicken restaurant, it was more like a pub! Like a pub.

255
00:23:52,380 --> 00:24:00,860
I remember cheering for Korea while eating chicken and beer there.

256
00:24:00,860 --> 00:24:04,880
But it's amazing, everyone!

257
00:24:04,880 --> 00:24:08,770
Korea won then.

258
00:24:08,770 --> 00:24:14,130
It was a match between Korea and Germany.

259
00:24:14,130 --> 00:24:16,930
But you all know.

260
00:24:16,930 --> 00:24:22,020
Germany is really a football powerhouse.

261
00:24:22,020 --> 00:24:24,790
Powerhouse! A strong country!

262
00:24:24,790 --> 00:24:28,230
But Korea won.

263
00:24:28,230 --> 00:24:29,950
Really amazing.

264
00:24:29,950 --> 00:24:34,460
The dopamine was no joke then.

265
00:24:34,460 --> 00:24:40,320
Wow, shouting, clapping, it was crazy.

266
00:24:40,320 --> 00:24:48,760
The really fun thing is, I came out after watching the whole game.

267
00:24:48,760 --> 00:24:50,740
I went outside.

268
00:24:50,740 --> 00:25:00,190
After watching the whole game, many people gave me high fives. High five!

269
00:25:00,190 --> 00:25:07,520
They said, "Congratulations Korea!" Many people said "Congratulations!" and left.

270
00:25:07,520 --> 00:25:16,080
I wasn't even a football player, but it felt good to be congratulated.

271
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:21,880
I just watched football, did nothing...

272
00:25:21,880 --> 00:25:31,680
But I could briefly feel like I was a football player.

273
00:25:31,680 --> 00:25:37,880
But I don't know how they knew I was Korean.

274
00:25:37,880 --> 00:25:39,900
Can you tell? Everyone?

275
00:25:39,900 --> 00:25:41,740
Can you tell?

276
00:25:41,740 --> 00:25:45,120
I guess it's obvious that I'm Korean.

277
00:25:45,120 --> 00:25:54,890
Whenever I go abroad besides Cambodia, I often hear "Annyeonghaseyo" (Hello in Korean).

278
00:25:54,890 --> 00:26:00,770
Of course, I also hear "Nihao" a lot...

279
00:26:00,770 --> 00:26:09,240
But these days, I think I hear "Annyeonghaseyo" more.

280
00:26:09,240 --> 00:26:13,220
"Annyeonghaseyo" and "Gamsahamnida" (Thank you).

281
00:26:13,220 --> 00:26:21,600
Do you know this is a big change for Korean people?

282
00:26:21,600 --> 00:26:24,460
A big change. Change.

283
00:26:24,460 --> 00:26:26,730
It means it has changed.

284
00:26:26,730 --> 00:26:28,290
Change.

285
00:26:28,290 --> 00:26:32,350
In the past, it was always "Nihao."

286
00:26:32,350 --> 00:26:34,780
When I passed by, people said "Nihao!"

287
00:26:34,780 --> 00:26:41,340
At first, it was really annoying, but now I'm used to it.

288
00:26:41,340 --> 00:26:46,780
Now I can just ignore it.

289
00:26:46,780 --> 00:26:51,140
The next thing I heard a lot was... "Konnichiwa."

290
00:26:51,140 --> 00:27:00,170
Ah, really, if you don't know which country someone is from, just say "Hi," right?

291
00:27:00,170 --> 00:27:05,190
Or you can ask. Small talk!

292
00:27:05,190 --> 00:27:10,490
You can ask, "Which country are you from?"

293
00:27:10,490 --> 00:27:24,260
On the other hand, Japanese and Chinese people would not feel good if they hear "Nihao," "Annyeonghaseyo," and "Konnichiwa" all mixed together.

294
00:27:24,260 --> 00:27:28,190
Ah, what should we talk about now?

295
00:27:28,190 --> 00:27:34,300
I think we've talked a lot about Korean football.

296
00:27:34,300 --> 00:27:46,240
We talked about the most famous football match in Korea, the coach, and the rival countries.

297
00:27:46,240 --> 00:27:47,730
Korea-Japan match.

298
00:27:47,730 --> 00:27:52,380
Now, I'll talk a bit about myself.

299
00:27:52,380 --> 00:28:00,030
First, there is a reason why I liked football when I was young.

300
00:28:00,030 --> 00:28:07,440
I really liked a player named Park Ji-sung from Korea.

301
00:28:07,440 --> 00:28:11,230
Do you guys know this player?

302
00:28:11,230 --> 00:28:19,520
First of all, this player is a legend, a legendary soccer player in Korea.

303
00:28:19,520 --> 00:28:23,100
He is now retired.

304
00:28:23,100 --> 00:28:24,640
Retired!

305
00:28:24,640 --> 00:28:27,170
It means he stopped playing.

306
00:28:27,170 --> 00:28:37,660
You might think, "He's not even a soccer player anymore, so why should I listen about him?"

307
00:28:37,660 --> 00:28:38,980
I understand.

308
00:28:38,980 --> 00:28:42,920
But please listen just a little bit.

309
00:28:42,920 --> 00:28:55,710
If you listen a little, you will understand why Koreans still think of Park Ji-sung as special.

310
00:28:55,710 --> 00:29:09,730
Nowadays, there are quite a few Korean players who have joined overseas soccer teams, like Son Heung-min or Lee Kang-in.

311
00:29:09,730 --> 00:29:11,020
Quite a few.

312
00:29:11,020 --> 00:29:16,580
But in the past, there weren't that many.

313
00:29:16,580 --> 00:29:25,710
But this player, Park Ji-sung, got into a really famous soccer team in England.

314
00:29:25,710 --> 00:29:31,740
He joined the English team Manchester United.

315
00:29:31,740 --> 00:29:35,310
He was the first Korean. The very first.

316
00:29:35,310 --> 00:29:40,340
The first Korean to join that team.

317
00:29:40,340 --> 00:29:43,870
That's what we call "the first."

318
00:29:43,870 --> 00:29:48,570
So Koreans were very happy.

319
00:29:48,570 --> 00:29:51,740
They felt proud.

320
00:29:51,740 --> 00:29:55,350
How proud do you think they were!

321
00:29:55,350 --> 00:30:02,410
Actually, at that time, Korean soccer wasn't very famous.

322
00:30:02,410 --> 00:30:11,820
Now, if I ask my English friends, they all know Son Heung-min.

323
00:30:11,820 --> 00:30:23,790
But back then, it was truly a miracle for a Korean player to join a famous English soccer team.

324
00:30:23,790 --> 00:30:25,180
A miracle.

325
00:30:25,180 --> 00:30:37,130
Photos came out of him practicing and playing with Cristiano Ronaldo, and people were really surprised.

326
00:30:37,130 --> 00:30:43,330
They were amazed to see these two on the same team!

327
00:30:43,330 --> 00:30:50,900
After that, many Korean players started playing soccer in Europe.

328
00:30:50,900 --> 00:30:58,340
Park Ji-sung was the one who paved the way.

329
00:30:58,340 --> 00:31:09,380
Just for that alone, he was already an amazing player, but the reason I like Park Ji-sung is different.

330
00:31:09,380 --> 00:31:16,250
It's because of the 2010 South Africa World Cup.

331
00:31:16,250 --> 00:31:20,950
Do you remember South Africa? I mentioned it earlier.

332
00:31:20,950 --> 00:31:25,270
Republic of South Africa, South Africa.

333
00:31:25,270 --> 00:31:38,090
I said earlier that the most impressive game for Koreans was the 2002 World Cup.

334
00:31:38,090 --> 00:31:44,880
But I also said I was too young to remember it at all.

335
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:50,630
Now, a World Cup I can remember has come.

336
00:31:50,630 --> 00:31:56,350
That is the 2010 South Africa World Cup.

337
00:31:56,350 --> 00:32:05,800
At the 2010 South Africa World Cup, Park Ji-sung was the captain of the Korean national team.

338
00:32:05,800 --> 00:32:08,080
Captain.

339
00:32:08,080 --> 00:32:12,960
He led the team very well.

340
00:32:12,960 --> 00:32:14,940
He led them well.

341
00:32:14,940 --> 00:32:25,910
So Korea went to the round of 16 for the first time in a World Cup held overseas.

342
00:32:25,910 --> 00:32:33,920
The 2002 World Cup wasn't held overseas.

343
00:32:33,920 --> 00:32:39,200
It was the Korea-Japan World Cup, so it wasn't overseas.

344
00:32:39,200 --> 00:32:46,260
But for the first time, Korea reached the round of 16 in an overseas World Cup.

345
00:32:46,260 --> 00:32:52,320
When I was young, I watched these World Cup games at school.

346
00:32:52,320 --> 00:32:59,240
I still vividly remember cheering with my friends.

347
00:32:59,240 --> 00:33:05,390
It was so fun, and the dopamine rush was incredible.

348
00:33:05,390 --> 00:33:13,700
Definitely, watching soccer with others is more fun.

349
00:33:13,700 --> 00:33:22,590
Watching and cheering together makes it more enjoyable.

350
00:33:22,590 --> 00:33:33,280
Honestly, I wanted to feel that kind of atmosphere in this World Cup too, but it's really disappointing.

351
00:33:33,280 --> 00:33:40,220
The atmosphere of wearing uniforms and cheering together with people...

352
00:33:40,220 --> 00:33:46,080
I wanted to feel that kind of atmosphere, but it's a shame.

353
00:33:46,080 --> 00:33:53,790
But recently, just a few days ago, I got a message from a friend.

354
00:33:53,790 --> 00:33:57,570
This friend is Egyptian.

355
00:33:57,570 --> 00:34:02,580
An Egyptian friend living in Dubai.

356
00:34:02,580 --> 00:34:07,390
Egypt won a World Cup match.

357
00:34:07,390 --> 00:34:08,800
They won.

358
00:34:08,800 --> 00:34:11,170
Who did they play against then?

359
00:34:11,170 --> 00:34:13,600
I think it was Australia.

360
00:34:13,600 --> 00:34:16,040
Egypt, Australia.

361
00:34:16,040 --> 00:34:20,470
But Egypt beat Australia!

362
00:34:20,470 --> 00:34:28,230
So that friend contacted me saying their country won.

363
00:34:28,230 --> 00:34:37,380
"We won!!!" like that...

364
00:34:37,380 --> 00:34:46,860
Right now, Koreans are so sad because they lost in the World Cup... Are they bragging now?

365
00:34:46,860 --> 00:34:49,510
Are they bragging?

366
00:34:49,510 --> 00:34:53,690
Are they fanning a burning house?

367
00:34:53,690 --> 00:34:59,390
Have you ever heard this phrase?

368
00:34:59,390 --> 00:35:02,370
"Fanning a burning house."

369
00:35:02,370 --> 00:35:06,940
This is a Korean proverb.

370
00:35:06,940 --> 00:35:08,290
Proverb.

371
00:35:08,290 --> 00:35:16,580
Wow, I think this is the first time I'm talking about a Korean proverb on my channel.

372
00:35:16,580 --> 00:35:17,790
Maybe?

373
00:35:17,790 --> 00:35:25,090
This phrase is used when you make an already bad situation worse.

374
00:35:25,090 --> 00:35:28,630
Here, there is a burning house.

375
00:35:28,630 --> 00:35:31,130
The house is on fire.

376
00:35:31,130 --> 00:35:35,990
But someone is fanning it with a fan.

377
00:35:35,990 --> 00:35:37,610
Fanning.

378
00:35:37,610 --> 00:35:40,060
This is fanning.

379
00:35:40,060 --> 00:35:45,220
Then the fire will get bigger, right?

380
00:35:45,220 --> 00:35:50,570
If you fan it like this, the fire will get bigger.

381
00:35:50,570 --> 00:35:54,940
It means making the problem bigger.

382
00:35:54,940 --> 00:35:58,210
Fanning a burning house.

383
00:35:58,210 --> 00:36:06,880
Korea lost... I'm very sad, but what is it that they're bragging?

384
00:36:06,880 --> 00:36:10,940
It makes me even sadder.

385
00:36:10,940 --> 00:36:16,290
So it's like fanning a burning house.

386
00:36:16,290 --> 00:36:17,440
Just kidding.

387
00:36:17,440 --> 00:36:20,440
I was just jealous, actually.

388
00:36:20,440 --> 00:36:25,090
So I told my friend I was jealous.

389
00:36:25,090 --> 00:36:29,180
In English, "Jealous. I'm so jealous."

390
00:36:29,180 --> 00:36:38,620
Did you know that the expression "Jealous" has two different expressions in Korean?

391
00:36:38,620 --> 00:36:43,140
There are two very commonly used expressions.

392
00:36:43,140 --> 00:36:46,570
The first one is "부럽다" (bureopda).

393
00:36:46,570 --> 00:36:49,760
"부럽다," "부러워요."

394
00:36:49,760 --> 00:36:57,470
Egypt won in soccer? I'm jealous, really jealous. Congratulations!

395
00:36:57,470 --> 00:37:00,760
What does feeling jealous mean?

396
00:37:00,760 --> 00:37:08,290
You use it when someone else has something good or something good happens to them.

397
00:37:08,290 --> 00:37:10,970
There is no bad feeling.

398
00:37:10,970 --> 00:37:15,780
You don't hate or dislike that person.

399
00:37:15,780 --> 00:37:21,030
You just want to be like that too.

400
00:37:21,030 --> 00:37:22,460
I'm jealous.

401
00:37:22,460 --> 00:37:28,780
Korea also wanted to win the World Cup... Ah, I'm jealous.

402
00:37:28,780 --> 00:37:33,310
Or, are you going on an overseas trip this time?

403
00:37:33,310 --> 00:37:34,850
Going abroad?

404
00:37:34,850 --> 00:37:37,650
Wow, I'm so jealous.

405
00:37:37,650 --> 00:37:44,430
You can say "I'm jealous" when you want to travel too.

406
00:37:44,430 --> 00:37:51,390
The second one is "질투 나다" / "질투 나요" (jil-tu nada / jil-tu nayo).

407
00:37:51,390 --> 00:37:55,580
This has a somewhat bad feeling.

408
00:37:55,580 --> 00:38:03,780
It's a bit sad... too jealous and a bit angry feeling.

409
00:38:03,780 --> 00:38:10,740
This phrase is mostly used in romantic relationships.

410
00:38:10,740 --> 00:38:18,610
A romantic relationship means dating a girlfriend or boyfriend.

411
00:38:18,610 --> 00:38:25,920
If your boyfriend keeps talking to another girl.

412
00:38:25,920 --> 00:38:31,990
He doesn't even answer your calls but talks to another girl.

413
00:38:31,990 --> 00:38:33,950
Then how do you feel?

414
00:38:33,950 --> 00:38:36,480
Very annoyed, right?

415
00:38:36,480 --> 00:38:37,980
Jealous.

416
00:38:37,980 --> 00:38:41,300
This is not just being envious, it's jealousy.

417
00:38:41,300 --> 00:38:45,280
It's like feeling you might lose this person.

418
00:38:45,280 --> 00:38:55,200
Or if my mom seems to love my older sister more than me.

419
00:38:55,200 --> 00:38:58,720
Then I feel jealous.

420
00:38:58,720 --> 00:39:04,540
Especially young kids feel this kind of jealousy a lot.

421
00:39:04,540 --> 00:39:11,900
So "부럽다" is a lighter word you can use.

422
00:39:11,900 --> 00:39:13,060
부럽다.

423
00:39:13,060 --> 00:39:19,740
Let's practice once more with me. Practice!

424
00:39:19,740 --> 00:39:26,750
For example, you have a friend who studies Korean with you.

425
00:39:26,750 --> 00:39:31,460
Of course, that friend is not Korean.

426
00:39:31,460 --> 00:39:38,240
But you both started at the same time, and that friend is really good.

427
00:39:38,240 --> 00:39:44,160
Then what can you say to your friend here?

428
00:39:44,160 --> 00:39:47,610
부럽다 vs 질투 나다?

429
00:39:47,610 --> 00:39:53,460
Quiz time, 3 2 1

430
00:39:53,460 --> 00:39:57,340
Yes, 부럽다. It's envy.

431
00:39:57,340 --> 00:40:01,990
Hey, I'm jealous. Why are you so good at Korean?

432
00:40:01,990 --> 00:40:05,060
Ah, I'm jealous. So jealous.

433
00:40:05,060 --> 00:40:07,720
I'm dying of jealousy!

434
00:40:07,720 --> 00:40:10,170
But, plot twist.

435
00:40:10,170 --> 00:40:13,500
There is a twist. A twist.

436
00:40:13,500 --> 00:40:18,370
You can also say "질투 난다" here.

437
00:40:18,370 --> 00:40:21,320
Huh? What does that mean?

438
00:40:21,320 --> 00:40:30,050
Jealousy is a feeling of being so envious that you're angry, but how do you use it with a friend?

439
00:40:30,050 --> 00:40:35,450
Koreans sometimes use this phrase jokingly.

440
00:40:35,450 --> 00:40:39,670
Ah, what? I'm jealous! Why are you so good at Korean?

441
00:40:39,670 --> 00:40:41,990
Ah, really jealous.

442
00:40:41,990 --> 00:40:45,930
If you say it like this with a smile, it's okay.

443
00:40:45,930 --> 00:40:56,000
Rather than saying "I'm envious," saying "I'm jealous" like this means you're really close friends! True friends.

444
00:40:56,000 --> 00:41:01,000
"True friends" is slang for really close friends.

445
00:41:01,000 --> 00:41:10,620
Originally, "질투 난다" is a very serious phrase in Korean, but they use it jokingly.

446
00:41:10,620 --> 00:41:14,300
Because they are really friends.

447
00:41:14,300 --> 00:41:17,980
Now you understand better, right everyone?

448
00:41:17,980 --> 00:41:25,480
Now when you hear "jealous" in English, you should immediately think of these two Korean expressions.

449
00:41:25,480 --> 00:41:27,560
First, 부럽다.

450
00:41:27,560 --> 00:41:30,300
Second, 질투 나다.

451
00:41:30,300 --> 00:41:36,770
In Korean, these two words feel quite different, don't they?

452
00:41:36,770 --> 00:41:45,830
So when Koreans learn the English word "jealous," they really get confused.

453
00:41:45,830 --> 00:41:48,230
I was very confused too.

454
00:41:48,230 --> 00:41:52,860
I was so confused that I still remember it.

455
00:41:52,860 --> 00:41:59,190
That's why I'm explaining it in detail here now.

456
00:41:59,190 --> 00:42:09,900
In the past, when my friend said "I'm so jealous" in English, sometimes it felt a bit unpleasant or awkward.

457
00:42:09,900 --> 00:42:11,650
It's really strange, right?

458
00:42:11,650 --> 00:42:19,520
For Koreans, the word "jealous" feels very strong.

459
00:42:19,520 --> 00:42:21,840
I don't know why.

460
00:42:21,840 --> 00:42:31,360
Probably because in the vocabulary books we studied as kids, it said "jealous" means "to feel jealousy"...

461
00:42:31,360 --> 00:42:35,540
Oh? Jealousy? Oh, that's kind of strong.

462
00:42:35,540 --> 00:42:40,860
Then how on earth do you say "to be envious" in English?

463
00:42:40,860 --> 00:42:48,170
I think many Koreans, including myself, have really thought about this.

464
00:42:48,170 --> 00:42:57,800
"To be envious" is a phrase Koreans use very often, but how do you say it in English? I was curious.

465
00:42:57,800 --> 00:43:01,520
So I looked it up on the internet.

466
00:43:01,520 --> 00:43:07,780
Back then, of course, there was no AI or artificial intelligence.

467
00:43:07,780 --> 00:43:15,240
At that time, we only had Naver and Papago.

468
00:43:15,240 --> 00:43:19,880
There was something called Naver Knowledge iN.

469
00:43:19,880 --> 00:43:27,840
Knowledge iN is a place where you ask questions directly to people.

470
00:43:27,840 --> 00:43:33,590
It's kind of like Reddit? Although I don't really know Reddit...

471
00:43:33,590 --> 00:43:40,760
Anyway, if you post a question, people answer it there.

472
00:43:40,760 --> 00:43:45,140
For example, "How do you say '부럽다' in English?"

473
00:43:45,140 --> 00:43:49,330
Then Korean people answer.

474
00:43:49,330 --> 00:43:55,470
Koreans ask and Koreans answer.

475
00:43:55,470 --> 00:44:00,800
It's a space where Koreans ask and answer questions.

476
00:44:00,800 --> 00:44:10,270
People there mostly said, "Jealous means 질투 나다 / Envy means 부럽다."

477
00:44:10,270 --> 00:44:20,450
But if you learn one Korean word as just one English word, it can feel a bit awkward sometimes.

478
00:44:20,450 --> 00:44:29,610
When Koreans learn "부럽다" in English, they often learn it as "envy."

479
00:44:29,610 --> 00:44:41,680
But in my opinion, when you talk with friends, you seem to use "jealous" more than "envy."

480
00:44:41,680 --> 00:44:48,660
"Jealous" seems like a word used more often in daily life.

481
00:44:48,660 --> 00:44:53,500
From this experience, I realized something.

482
00:44:53,500 --> 00:45:05,890
I think it's more important to learn from sentences that native speakers actually use than just vocabulary books with pictures, words, and meanings.

483
00:45:05,890 --> 00:45:12,150
For example, I watch American dramas.

484
00:45:12,150 --> 00:45:20,690
In those dramas, a friend smiles and says to another friend, "I'm so jealous."

485
00:45:20,690 --> 00:45:25,620
And the subtitle shows "I'm so jealous."

486
00:45:25,620 --> 00:45:30,800
Then you can immediately understand the situation.

487
00:45:30,800 --> 00:45:36,150
Ah, in this situation, they use "jealous."

488
00:45:36,150 --> 00:45:48,090
Even without subtitles, just by seeing the smiling face or hearing it, you can understand right away.

489
00:45:48,090 --> 00:45:55,990
Learning with the situation makes it easier to remember.

490
00:45:55,990 --> 00:46:03,560
But if you just memorize words and meanings, you don't know when to use them well.

491
00:46:03,560 --> 00:46:10,540
So you might use unnatural expressions.

492
00:46:10,540 --> 00:46:20,500
I hope you watch a lot of Korean TV programs and listen to many podcasts.

493
00:46:20,500 --> 00:46:27,670
When you watch dramas, you can immediately understand what situation it is.

494
00:46:27,670 --> 00:46:37,810
So you can naturally learn, "Oh, in this situation, they use this Korean expression!"

495
00:46:37,810 --> 00:46:41,050
It's the same with podcasts.

496
00:46:41,050 --> 00:46:42,210
It's the same.

497
00:46:42,210 --> 00:46:46,230
Podcasts always have a topic.

498
00:46:46,230 --> 00:46:47,270
Topic.

499
00:46:47,270 --> 00:46:56,240
For example, last week I made a podcast about summer in Europe and Korea.

500
00:46:56,240 --> 00:47:02,490
Then you can tell just by looking at the title and thumbnail.

501
00:47:02,490 --> 00:47:06,760
Ah, today's about summer.

502
00:47:06,760 --> 00:47:11,130
I'm going to learn expressions about summer weather in Korea.

503
00:47:11,130 --> 00:47:16,150
You can think about the content beforehand like this.

504
00:47:16,150 --> 00:47:25,070
Since you already know the topic, it's easier to understand Korean.

505
00:47:25,070 --> 00:47:29,900
It sticks in your memory better.

506
00:47:29,900 --> 00:47:33,430
Korean has a characteristic.

507
00:47:33,430 --> 00:47:42,190
In Korean, one word is often used with many different meanings.

508
00:47:42,190 --> 00:47:47,770
Words that have the same pronunciation but different meanings.

509
00:47:47,770 --> 00:47:56,210
So many times, words you already know are used with different meanings.

510
00:47:56,210 --> 00:48:01,880
Very simple words you already know.

511
00:48:01,880 --> 00:48:07,720
For example, the word "치다" (chida).

512
00:48:07,720 --> 00:48:10,000
You play tennis (테니스를 쳐요).

513
00:48:10,000 --> 00:48:13,050
You hit a friend with your fist (친구를 주먹으로 쳐요).

514
00:48:13,050 --> 00:48:15,800
Of course, that's not okay, right?

515
00:48:15,800 --> 00:48:18,550
Thunder strikes (천둥번개가 쳐요).

516
00:48:18,550 --> 00:48:22,870
It rains, thunder and lightning strike.

517
00:48:22,870 --> 00:48:25,060
Waves hit (파도가 쳐요).

518
00:48:25,060 --> 00:48:31,290
It's the same "치다," but the meanings are a bit different.

519
00:48:31,290 --> 00:48:40,920
When learning weather, you learn expressions like "천둥 치다," "번개 치다," "파도 치다" together.

520
00:48:40,920 --> 00:48:49,350
But if you only memorize the word "치다" from a vocabulary book, there are too many meanings.

521
00:48:49,350 --> 00:48:56,500
And if you memorize only by looking, it's hard to remember for a long time.

522
00:48:56,500 --> 00:49:01,690
So when you watch dramas or podcasts,

523
00:49:01,690 --> 00:49:09,210
and think, "Ah, this word came up again!" and learn it, it's more fun.

524
00:49:09,210 --> 00:49:16,680
First, practice using basic words in various situations.

525
00:49:16,680 --> 00:49:27,670
As you get used to the feeling and nuance of Korean, then you can learn difficult words.

526
00:49:27,670 --> 00:49:32,890
Because this is real everyday Korean!

527
00:49:32,890 --> 00:49:39,570
Of course, some Koreans use many difficult words.

528
00:49:39,570 --> 00:49:41,400
Smart people.

529
00:49:41,400 --> 00:49:52,130
But in daily life, people often use basic words in many situations.

530
00:49:52,130 --> 00:50:05,390
So you should also practice using these words freely and properly in various situations.

531
00:50:05,390 --> 00:50:12,980
Please make sure to review the expressions and words that come up in the podcasts.

532
00:50:12,980 --> 00:50:21,410
Listen, understand, speak, and try writing your own sentences. Got it?

533
00:50:21,410 --> 00:50:23,710
Really, promise!

534
00:50:23,710 --> 00:50:28,790
Wait, what were we talking about?

535
00:50:28,790 --> 00:50:38,330
Wait, today's topic is about soccer, but suddenly we talked a lot about Korean.

536
00:50:38,330 --> 00:50:41,290
But isn't it fun?

537
00:50:41,290 --> 00:50:53,180
On days when I decide to make a long podcast, I think it's good because I can talk about more diverse topics.

538
00:50:53,180 --> 00:51:02,470
For example, the summer weather podcast I uploaded last week already had a set topic.

539
00:51:02,470 --> 00:51:12,990
And I thought the video length should be within 20 minutes.

540
00:51:12,990 --> 00:51:18,500
So I only talked about really important weather topics.

541
00:51:18,500 --> 00:51:24,150
But one hour is really a long time.

542
00:51:24,150 --> 00:51:31,750
Today, I feel like I'm talking more freely with you all.

543
00:51:31,750 --> 00:51:37,170
Anyway, what was I talking about?

544
00:51:37,170 --> 00:51:39,450
I was talking about being envious.

545
00:51:39,450 --> 00:51:46,610
I was really envious that my Egyptian friend won at the World Cup.

546
00:51:46,610 --> 00:51:54,230
My friend sent me a video, and I watched it; they were doing penalty kicks.

547
00:51:54,230 --> 00:51:55,830
Penalty kicks.

548
00:51:55,830 --> 00:51:59,670
The thrill of penalty kicks!

549
00:51:59,670 --> 00:52:03,440
Do you know what penalty kicks are?

550
00:52:03,440 --> 00:52:11,700
When a soccer match ends and the score is tied, what do they do?

551
00:52:11,700 --> 00:52:18,790
Players take turns kicking the ball one by one to decide the winning team.

552
00:52:18,790 --> 00:52:22,450
That's exactly what a penalty shootout is.

553
00:52:22,450 --> 00:52:27,910
Wow, penalty shootouts really seem full of thrill.

554
00:52:27,910 --> 00:52:30,500
They seem really thrilling.

555
00:52:30,500 --> 00:52:36,110
It was a really short video, but I was so immersed.

556
00:52:36,110 --> 00:52:37,850
I was really absorbed.

557
00:52:37,850 --> 00:52:40,930
It really looked fun.

558
00:52:40,930 --> 00:52:45,270
Okay, let's get back to my story.

559
00:52:45,270 --> 00:52:49,800
Earlier, I was talking about player Park Ji-sung, right?

560
00:52:49,800 --> 00:52:57,810
I said I watched the South Africa World Cup very impressively and enjoyably.

561
00:52:57,810 --> 00:53:09,730
I liked him even more because Park Ji-sung was the first Korean to join a famous English football team.

562
00:53:09,730 --> 00:53:15,110
But there is one more important reason.

563
00:53:15,110 --> 00:53:19,650
Park Ji-sung is from my neighborhood.

564
00:53:19,650 --> 00:53:24,130
So I think I cheered for him even more.

565
00:53:24,130 --> 00:53:32,250
Don't you also feel like cheering more when someone from your neighborhood does well?

566
00:53:32,250 --> 00:53:35,760
Also, there's something really amazing.

567
00:53:35,760 --> 00:53:41,780
There is even a road named after Park Ji-sung. A road.

568
00:53:41,780 --> 00:53:43,770
Park Ji-sung Road.

569
00:53:43,770 --> 00:53:51,450
So when you drive in Korea, you see Park Ji-sung's name.

570
00:53:51,450 --> 00:53:53,080
Isn't that amazing?

571
00:53:53,080 --> 00:53:57,970
Does your country have something like this?

572
00:53:57,970 --> 00:53:59,740
Is there something like this?

573
00:53:59,740 --> 00:54:05,310
Are there places named after famous people?

574
00:54:05,310 --> 00:54:13,910
What comes to my mind right now is Chopin Airport in Warsaw, Poland.

575
00:54:13,910 --> 00:54:15,700
Chopin Airport.

576
00:54:15,700 --> 00:54:21,940
When I first heard that name, I thought it was really cool.

577
00:54:21,940 --> 00:54:29,950
But Chopin is... Mr. Chopin? Should I say Mr. Chopin? (lol)

578
00:54:29,950 --> 00:54:33,590
Chopin is a historical figure.

579
00:54:33,590 --> 00:54:37,880
A great person who has already passed away.

580
00:54:37,880 --> 00:54:44,950
A great person means someone many people respect and who is famous.

581
00:54:44,950 --> 00:54:54,770
But Park Ji-sung is still alive, yet a road was named after him like this.

582
00:54:54,770 --> 00:54:57,970
That's really an amazing thing.

583
00:54:57,970 --> 00:55:06,920
As far as I remember, at first there were people who opposed this road.

584
00:55:06,920 --> 00:55:09,050
People who opposed it.

585
00:55:09,050 --> 00:55:20,170
They said, "I know he's a great player, but do we really have to change the road name?"

586
00:55:20,170 --> 00:55:24,300
This road was made a long time ago.

587
00:55:24,300 --> 00:55:33,660
So now it has become a road name that everyone naturally uses.

588
00:55:33,660 --> 00:55:42,890
Now, you can feel how famous Park Ji-sung was in Korea, right?

589
00:55:42,890 --> 00:55:49,690
I am a fan of Park Ji-sung, so I even went to watch his retirement match.

590
00:55:49,690 --> 00:55:51,370
Retirement match.

591
00:55:51,370 --> 00:55:53,180
Last match.

592
00:55:53,180 --> 00:55:57,420
The last match playing as a football player.

593
00:55:57,420 --> 00:56:00,880
This is called a retirement match.

594
00:56:00,880 --> 00:56:05,170
At that time, I felt really strange.

595
00:56:05,170 --> 00:56:13,850
Actually, while preparing for this podcast, I tidied up my room a bit.

596
00:56:13,850 --> 00:56:23,150
And! I found a diary I wrote when Park Ji-sung retired.

597
00:56:23,150 --> 00:56:25,850
I found it. Ta-da!

598
00:56:25,850 --> 00:56:32,910
By the way, I bought this diary in Shanghai, China.

599
00:56:32,910 --> 00:56:37,210
It has a vintage feel, so it's really pretty, right?

600
00:56:37,210 --> 00:56:44,940
But I'm too embarrassed to show this diary to you all.

601
00:56:44,940 --> 00:56:47,970
It's a bit awkward.

602
00:56:47,970 --> 00:56:56,500
I wrote it when I was young, so my writing isn't good and my handwriting is not pretty at all.

603
00:56:56,500 --> 00:57:00,950
My favorite part is this part.

604
00:57:00,950 --> 00:57:06,290
Park Ji-sung is always humble.

605
00:57:06,290 --> 00:57:10,060
He doesn't get arrogant easily.

606
00:57:10,060 --> 00:57:17,250
There is so much to learn from this player.

607
00:57:17,250 --> 00:57:28,410
I can't believe I can no longer see the player I respect wearing the uniform and playing.

608
00:57:28,410 --> 00:57:31,730
It had content like this.

609
00:57:31,730 --> 00:57:38,170
By the way, when writing diaries in Korean, sentences end with '-da' like this.

610
00:57:38,170 --> 00:57:42,770
On the next page, there is a short letter too.

611
00:57:42,770 --> 00:57:46,450
I'll just read the last part.

612
00:57:46,450 --> 00:57:54,040
Thank you so much for all your hard work, and I am proud to be Korean!

613
00:57:54,040 --> 00:58:03,020
Park Ji-sung will be remembered as a hero of Korean football fans for life.

614
00:58:03,020 --> 00:58:10,040
I will also follow Park Ji-sung and become a wonderful light!

615
00:58:10,040 --> 00:58:13,140
Thank you and I love you.

616
00:58:13,140 --> 00:58:17,010
I will see you soon in Suwon.

617
00:58:17,010 --> 00:58:18,540
Moved.

618
00:58:18,540 --> 00:58:26,990
After writing this diary and letter, I actually went to watch the last match.

619
00:58:26,990 --> 00:58:32,970
I was really glad I could watch that match in person.

620
00:58:32,970 --> 00:58:38,570
After that, I really started to like football.

621
00:58:38,570 --> 00:58:44,730
And I got so into football that I started watching overseas football too.

622
00:58:44,730 --> 00:58:52,270
I came to like a team called Real Madrid from Spain.

623
00:58:52,270 --> 00:58:58,580
I don't really remember why I started liking this team.

624
00:58:58,580 --> 00:59:04,820
There is a time difference between Korea and Spain.

625
00:59:04,820 --> 00:59:06,380
Time difference.

626
00:59:06,380 --> 00:59:13,660
So I often stayed up until dawn waiting to watch the matches.

627
00:59:13,660 --> 00:59:19,480
I even set D-day reminders on my phone.

628
00:59:19,480 --> 00:59:23,950
8 days until the English league starts.

629
00:59:23,950 --> 00:59:28,050
10 days until the Spanish league starts.

630
00:59:28,050 --> 00:59:34,580
And there is a very big tournament called the Champions League.

631
00:59:34,580 --> 00:59:38,430
Anyone who likes football knows this, right?

632
00:59:38,430 --> 00:59:44,480
At that time, the team I supported won the championship.

633
00:59:44,480 --> 00:59:49,830
Wow, I still remember how I felt then.

634
00:59:49,830 --> 00:59:54,670
Recently, I traveled to Japan.

635
00:59:54,670 --> 00:59:58,520
There, I met a Spanish friend.

636
00:59:58,520 --> 01:00:09,220
I thought about what to talk about with that friend, and since he was Spanish, I talked about the Real Madrid football team.

637
01:00:09,220 --> 01:00:14,240
But these days, I don't watch football.

638
01:00:14,240 --> 01:00:17,940
Still, I talked about players I knew from before!

639
01:00:17,940 --> 01:00:19,660
From the time I watched.

640
01:00:19,660 --> 01:00:27,250
I talked about players like Ramos, Özil, Iker Casillas.

641
01:00:27,250 --> 01:00:32,690
Then that friend was really surprised.

642
01:00:32,690 --> 01:00:39,300
He said, "How do you know these players?"

643
01:00:39,300 --> 01:00:46,410
So we laughed a lot and had a fun conversation.

644
01:00:46,410 --> 01:00:57,390
Also, when I was young and liked football, I often went to watch football matches in Korea.

645
01:00:57,390 --> 01:01:00,690
This is called "direct viewing."

646
01:01:00,690 --> 01:01:02,390
Direct viewing.

647
01:01:02,390 --> 01:01:09,380
Going to the stadium to watch the game in person is called direct viewing.

648
01:01:09,380 --> 01:01:11,450
Football direct viewing.

649
01:01:11,450 --> 01:01:13,530
Baseball direct viewing.

650
01:01:13,530 --> 01:01:19,250
Do you also like going to watch games in person?

651
01:01:19,250 --> 01:01:25,640
What sports games are popular in your country?

652
01:01:25,640 --> 01:01:31,100
In Korea, baseball is really popular these days.

653
01:01:31,100 --> 01:01:37,750
But I hardly know about baseball and only liked soccer.

654
01:01:37,750 --> 01:01:44,010
One time, I went to watch a really special soccer match.

655
01:01:44,010 --> 01:01:47,540
It was a charity match.

656
01:01:47,540 --> 01:01:55,990
A charity match is a special game held to help people in need.

657
01:01:55,990 --> 01:02:01,370
Do you know who organized this charity match?

658
01:02:01,370 --> 01:02:05,360
It was Coach Hong Myung-bo.

659
01:02:05,360 --> 01:02:14,230
If you followed the recent Korean World Cup closely, you probably know this coach.

660
01:02:14,230 --> 01:02:24,350
He received a lot of criticism in Korea because of the 2026 World Cup.

661
01:02:24,350 --> 01:02:26,550
He was criticized.

662
01:02:26,550 --> 01:02:34,490
Actually, I don’t look at social media much when I’m resting.

663
01:02:34,490 --> 01:02:46,550
So I don’t immediately know what people from other countries are saying about Korea or what issues are trending.

664
01:02:46,550 --> 01:02:51,060
But there is one YouTuber I subscribe to.

665
01:02:51,060 --> 01:02:59,380
That YouTuber tells me what foreigners are currently saying about Korea.

666
01:02:59,380 --> 01:03:11,110
So instead of constantly checking social media, I watch that YouTuber’s videos when I want a summary.

667
01:03:11,110 --> 01:03:19,680
For example, they tell me what kind of interest foreigners have in Korea right now.

668
01:03:19,680 --> 01:03:23,730
But that YouTuber said something like this:

669
01:03:23,730 --> 01:03:29,220
Foreigners are currently criticizing Korea.

670
01:03:29,220 --> 01:03:34,120
They say Koreans are too sensitive.

671
01:03:34,120 --> 01:03:40,990
They think Korea lost the World Cup because of Coach Hong Myung-bo...

672
01:03:40,990 --> 01:03:46,950
And that the people, the Koreans, are harassing the coach.

673
01:03:46,950 --> 01:03:55,190
They also say it’s understandable to lose a game, so they don’t know why people are so angry.

674
01:03:55,190 --> 01:04:04,440
There is a culture in Korea where if the team loses, people criticize the coach and players.

675
01:04:04,440 --> 01:04:10,410
They say Koreans are too sensitive.

676
01:04:10,410 --> 01:04:15,140
But everyone, this is really a misunderstanding.

677
01:04:15,140 --> 01:04:21,210
We are not angry because we lost the World Cup.

678
01:04:21,210 --> 01:04:25,860
And we don’t hate the players.

679
01:04:25,860 --> 01:04:29,330
The players did really well.

680
01:04:29,330 --> 01:04:33,600
What fault could the players have?

681
01:04:33,600 --> 01:04:42,100
As I said earlier, Korean soccer has had many problems for a long time.

682
01:04:42,100 --> 01:04:48,280
Explaining all of this would be too long and complicated.

683
01:04:48,280 --> 01:04:51,350
But I mentioned earlier.

684
01:04:51,350 --> 01:04:59,800
When Dutch coach Hiddink came, many of those problems changed a lot.

685
01:04:59,800 --> 01:05:04,790
But now, the coach is Korean again.

686
01:05:04,790 --> 01:05:10,420
So these problems have come up again.

687
01:05:10,420 --> 01:05:15,010
That’s why Korean people are angry.

688
01:05:15,010 --> 01:05:20,630
It’s not simply because they lost the game.

689
01:05:20,630 --> 01:05:26,720
I hope you don’t misunderstand this part.

690
01:05:26,720 --> 01:05:35,670
Anyway, Coach Hong Myung-bo’s image is really bad in Korea right now.

691
01:05:35,670 --> 01:05:42,290
But it wasn’t always this bad.

692
01:05:42,290 --> 01:05:51,030
So I brought this uniform to film this video...

693
01:05:51,030 --> 01:05:55,050
This was from Coach Hong Myung-bo’s match.

694
01:05:55,050 --> 01:06:07,090
Back then, it was really fun, but now Coach Hong Myung-bo’s image has changed a lot. I thought that was interesting.

695
01:06:07,090 --> 01:06:11,280
You really never know what will happen in life.

696
01:06:11,280 --> 01:06:15,590
You really never know what will happen in life.

697
01:06:15,590 --> 01:06:23,650
This means no one knows what will happen in the future.

698
01:06:23,650 --> 01:06:31,460
No one can know what will happen to a person in the future.

699
01:06:31,460 --> 01:06:34,100
You never know what will happen.

700
01:06:34,100 --> 01:06:38,220
You really never know what will happen. Right?

701
01:06:38,220 --> 01:06:49,300
Even a coach who was so famous and popular now has a very different image.

702
01:06:49,300 --> 01:06:59,680
By the way, Coach Hong Myung-bo was the captain of the 2002 World Cup team.

703
01:06:59,680 --> 01:07:03,870
I mentioned this earlier, right?

704
01:07:03,870 --> 01:07:13,170
The 2002 World Cup is a legendary World Cup that Koreans will remember for life.

705
01:07:13,170 --> 01:07:21,200
Since he was the captain of that team, his image must have been very good back then.

706
01:07:21,200 --> 01:07:31,190
But now it’s like this... Wow, you really never know what will happen in life.

707
01:07:31,190 --> 01:07:36,900
Anyway, now I will explain about this uniform.

708
01:07:36,900 --> 01:07:46,680
As I said earlier, this is the uniform the players wore in the charity match.

709
01:07:46,680 --> 01:07:50,290
A lot of players came.

710
01:07:50,290 --> 01:07:56,850
K League! K League is the name of Korea’s soccer league.

711
01:07:56,850 --> 01:08:03,870
Various players from different K League teams participated.

712
01:08:03,870 --> 01:08:05,160
They came.

713
01:08:05,160 --> 01:08:18,730
Usually, you can’t see so many players in one game, so for soccer fans like me, it was a really great match.

714
01:08:18,730 --> 01:08:27,600
But after the game, one player threw his uniform into the stands.

715
01:08:27,600 --> 01:08:29,320
The stands.

716
01:08:29,320 --> 01:08:39,850
You’ve probably seen players throw their uniforms to fans when watching soccer, right?

717
01:08:39,850 --> 01:08:43,610
I always only saw that on TV.

718
01:08:43,610 --> 01:08:50,310
But it actually happened right in front of me.

719
01:08:50,310 --> 01:08:54,870
And that uniform... flew to me.

720
01:08:54,870 --> 01:08:58,240
It was really amazing.

721
01:08:58,240 --> 01:09:03,410
How could something like this happen?

722
01:09:03,410 --> 01:09:11,160
By pure luck, that player threw the uniform to me.

723
01:09:11,160 --> 01:09:16,450
Suddenly, many people looked at me.

724
01:09:16,450 --> 01:09:26,890
And people came and asked, “Can I take a picture with this uniform?”

725
01:09:26,890 --> 01:09:31,860
So I took pictures with them.

726
01:09:31,860 --> 01:09:37,110
This uniform is still at my house.

727
01:09:37,110 --> 01:09:45,990
But honestly, everyone! Have you ever thought about this honestly?

728
01:09:45,990 --> 01:09:57,890
Soccer players run a lot and sweat a lot, so how do they give those uniforms to fans?

729
01:09:57,890 --> 01:10:01,220
I was always curious about that.

730
01:10:01,220 --> 01:10:09,770
But as soon as I got this uniform, it smelled really strongly of perfume.

731
01:10:09,770 --> 01:10:14,070
I didn’t smell it on purpose.

732
01:10:14,070 --> 01:10:16,630
I’m not a dog! (LOL)

733
01:10:16,630 --> 01:10:18,770
I’m not a weird person either.

734
01:10:18,770 --> 01:10:25,020
The perfume smell was really strong right when I got it.

735
01:10:25,020 --> 01:10:29,680
Please don’t misunderstand! I’m not a weird person (LOL).

736
01:10:29,680 --> 01:10:37,590
By the way, the player who threw this uniform, Jeong Dae-se, was born in Japan.

737
01:10:37,590 --> 01:10:40,120
He grew up in Japan.

738
01:10:40,120 --> 01:10:48,150
But he didn’t play for the Korean national team; he played for the North Korean national soccer team.

739
01:10:48,150 --> 01:10:55,920
And he also played in the Korean K League for a long time.

740
01:10:55,920 --> 01:10:58,820
It’s a bit complicated, right?

741
01:10:58,820 --> 01:11:07,560
If you’re curious about this, it might be good to look it up later.

742
01:11:07,560 --> 01:11:12,390
Now, let’s talk about something a bit more serious.

743
01:11:12,390 --> 01:11:16,370
It’s about my life.

744
01:11:16,370 --> 01:11:22,600
So far, I’ve talked quite a bit about soccer.

745
01:11:22,600 --> 01:11:33,060
I talked about why I started liking soccer, which teams I liked, and what matches I watched.

746
01:11:33,060 --> 01:11:40,970
Just from this, you can feel that I really liked soccer, right?

747
01:11:40,970 --> 01:11:47,510
I think I really liked sports players when I was young.

748
01:11:47,510 --> 01:12:04,010
Especially the players I mentioned several times today: Park Ji-sung, figure skater Kim Yuna, and volleyball player Kim Yeon-koung are my favorites.

749
01:12:04,010 --> 01:12:11,270
When I watch sports, I feel like the players are overcoming their limits.

750
01:12:11,270 --> 01:12:12,430
Limits!

751
01:12:12,430 --> 01:12:17,720
Limits mean you can’t go any further.

752
01:12:17,720 --> 01:12:21,350
Up to here... this is the end. It’s hard.

753
01:12:21,350 --> 01:12:23,240
This is the limit.

754
01:12:23,240 --> 01:12:31,650
But when I watch sports games, I feel like the athletes are surpassing their own limits.

755
01:12:31,650 --> 01:12:38,360
And I get a strength that makes me feel like I can do anything.

756
01:12:38,360 --> 01:12:42,810
I get really inspired.

757
01:12:42,810 --> 01:12:52,010
Especially when I was young, it was the time when Park Ji-sung was actively playing.

758
01:12:52,010 --> 01:12:57,480
At that time, I was not yet an adult.

759
01:12:57,480 --> 01:13:03,930
What kind of books did you read a lot when you were young?

760
01:13:03,930 --> 01:13:05,740
Biographies of great people.

761
01:13:05,740 --> 01:13:09,630
I read a lot of biographies of great people.

762
01:13:09,630 --> 01:13:12,950
The school told us to read them.

763
01:13:12,950 --> 01:13:19,890
Earlier, when I talked about Chopin, I explained the word "great person," right?

764
01:13:19,890 --> 01:13:28,200
A great person means someone who many people respect and who is famous.

765
01:13:28,200 --> 01:13:35,800
And a biography is a book that contains stories of respect about such people.

766
01:13:35,800 --> 01:13:42,990
When you read these books, you often find many stories about that person's childhood.

767
01:13:42,990 --> 01:13:52,960
For example, Park Ji-sung was shorter and thinner than others.

768
01:13:52,960 --> 01:14:01,620
So he often heard that "it would be difficult to become a professional player."

769
01:14:01,620 --> 01:14:13,690
But he trained very hard to overcome his shortcomings and became a great player, that's the story.

770
01:14:13,690 --> 01:14:18,660
And Park Ji-sung had flat feet.

771
01:14:18,660 --> 01:14:20,050
Flat feet.

772
01:14:20,050 --> 01:14:26,280
Flat feet means the middle of the sole is flat.

773
01:14:26,280 --> 01:14:30,100
That's not good for a soccer player.

774
01:14:30,100 --> 01:14:39,670
But Park Ji-sung overcame all of these and became an excellent soccer player.

775
01:14:39,670 --> 01:14:45,600
I learned a lot from these stories.

776
01:14:45,600 --> 01:14:56,730
I learned how to live life, what kind of mindset to have... and I wanted to resemble that kind of attitude.

777
01:14:56,730 --> 01:15:08,690
Especially, when you are young, your self! Your self is not fully formed yet.

778
01:15:08,690 --> 01:15:09,980
Self.

779
01:15:09,980 --> 01:15:16,950
Often, you don't really know what you like or what you want to do.

780
01:15:16,950 --> 01:15:25,880
So many people like sports players or respect celebrities.

781
01:15:25,880 --> 01:15:31,470
Who you like varies from person to person though.

782
01:15:31,470 --> 01:15:41,650
But at some point, I think this feeling of mine gradually disappeared.

783
01:15:41,650 --> 01:15:46,210
I really don't remember when it started.

784
01:15:46,210 --> 01:15:59,090
Going to watch soccer games in person, staying up all night to watch overseas soccer matches—all of that ended at some point.

785
01:15:59,090 --> 01:16:05,540
When I became an adult, there were so many things I had to care about.

786
01:16:05,540 --> 01:16:09,220
After graduating from college, I have to get a job...

787
01:16:09,220 --> 01:16:12,920
To get a job, I have to prepare for interviews...

788
01:16:12,920 --> 01:16:17,910
After college, when will I meet my friends?...

789
01:16:17,910 --> 01:16:20,930
When will I quit my job?

790
01:16:20,930 --> 01:16:27,450
Ah, I really like traveling... When will I be able to travel?

791
01:16:27,450 --> 01:16:35,440
Then I even thought about trying YouTube.

792
01:16:35,440 --> 01:16:44,420
With all these thoughts, my mind became really complicated.

793
01:16:44,420 --> 01:16:55,650
And after going through those times, now I am here talking with you all.

794
01:16:55,650 --> 01:17:08,020
Of course, there are people who manage to take care of busy things well and still enjoy sports.

795
01:17:08,020 --> 01:17:12,460
But I don't think I was able to do that.

796
01:17:12,460 --> 01:17:15,470
I think I didn't have any leisure.

797
01:17:15,470 --> 01:17:20,300
There were too many things to worry about.

798
01:17:20,300 --> 01:17:31,170
I used to laugh and enjoy watching games, and there were sports players I always respected.

799
01:17:31,170 --> 01:17:41,160
But at some point, I started focusing more on myself rather than those players.

800
01:17:41,160 --> 01:17:46,440
This might sound like a serious story.

801
01:17:46,440 --> 01:17:52,200
But I think many people can relate.

802
01:17:52,200 --> 01:17:57,840
I recently had a similar conversation with a friend.

803
01:17:57,840 --> 01:18:03,970
That friend really liked K-pop in high school.

804
01:18:03,970 --> 01:18:10,940
Just like I liked soccer, that friend liked K-pop.

805
01:18:10,940 --> 01:18:17,140
So they watched a lot of videos and bought albums.

806
01:18:17,140 --> 01:18:23,300
But recently, we had a similar conversation.

807
01:18:23,300 --> 01:18:28,920
We said that the passion we had before is no longer there.

808
01:18:28,920 --> 01:18:32,840
The passion seems to have disappeared.

809
01:18:32,840 --> 01:18:36,930
By the way, that friend is a foreigner.

810
01:18:36,930 --> 01:18:40,990
That friend said to me,

811
01:18:40,990 --> 01:18:48,290
Now I want to keep that passion as a childhood memory.

812
01:18:48,290 --> 01:18:57,370
This doesn't mean we don't watch sports or listen to music at all.

813
01:18:57,370 --> 01:19:06,180
It means the direction of that passion has changed compared to before.

814
01:19:06,180 --> 01:19:10,330
You all know what I mean, right?

815
01:19:10,330 --> 01:19:23,380
And this year, I also got a lot of new hobbies. I'm doing chess, nail art, and decorating diaries.

816
01:19:23,380 --> 01:19:28,780
Making things with my hands is so much fun.

817
01:19:28,780 --> 01:19:35,480
Nail art and diary decorating are fun because of the design aspect.

818
01:19:35,480 --> 01:19:45,270
I think about what colors to use, choose colors, and the process of making it myself is fun.

819
01:19:45,270 --> 01:19:46,550
Chess.

820
01:19:46,550 --> 01:19:50,170
Chess is fun because it makes you think.

821
01:19:50,170 --> 01:20:00,090
Of course, it's not that I'm good at chess... I do feel a bit embarrassed about using my brain.

822
01:20:00,090 --> 01:20:06,520
Still, chess really has the power to make you focus.

823
01:20:06,520 --> 01:20:13,570
When I go to a chess meeting, people usually talk to each other first.

824
01:20:13,570 --> 01:20:19,650
"Did you have a good weekend? What did you do? Did you practice chess?"

825
01:20:19,650 --> 01:20:29,790
We chat casually like this, but once the chess game starts, everyone becomes quiet.

826
01:20:29,790 --> 01:20:33,070
No one talks.

827
01:20:33,070 --> 01:20:36,590
It becomes a really quiet atmosphere.

828
01:20:36,590 --> 01:20:42,770
And I really like that focused time.

829
01:20:42,770 --> 01:20:49,550
Of course, there are many days I lose, so it's not always good.

830
01:20:49,550 --> 01:21:01,230
So now, rather than just watching as a hobby, I've become more interested in hobbies I can do myself.

831
01:21:01,230 --> 01:21:09,780
Among you, there are probably many who like both playing and watching soccer.

832
01:21:09,780 --> 01:21:15,930
Anyway, I don't remember when I changed like this.

833
01:21:15,930 --> 01:21:19,490
But this is how it happened.

834
01:21:19,490 --> 01:21:26,960
Among the students I teach, there is one who really loves soccer.

835
01:21:26,960 --> 01:21:31,700
A student who cries and laughs because of soccer.

836
01:21:31,700 --> 01:21:37,760
Actually, I watched this World Cup thanks to that student.

837
01:21:37,760 --> 01:21:42,780
It was really nice to watch soccer after a long time.

838
01:21:42,780 --> 01:21:51,090
I thought, "Right, this is why I liked soccer!"

839
01:21:51,090 --> 01:21:55,730
It reminded me a lot of the old days.

840
01:21:55,730 --> 01:21:59,860
And I missed those times a lot.

841
01:21:59,860 --> 01:22:05,650
The times when I purely cheered for the World Cup with friends.

842
01:22:05,650 --> 01:22:11,810
The time when I went to watch Park Ji-sung's retirement match.

843
01:22:11,810 --> 01:22:17,300
The time when Real Madrid won the Champions League.

844
01:22:17,300 --> 01:22:21,140
Ah, but I was really happy.

845
01:22:21,140 --> 01:22:29,490
Saying this makes me sound like a grandmother or grandfather telling old stories.

846
01:22:29,490 --> 01:22:35,120
Like, "When I was young..." that kind of feeling.

847
01:22:35,120 --> 01:22:42,060
But I really experienced a lot growing up in Korea.

848
01:22:42,060 --> 01:22:55,210
And I think these experiences are not just my own story, but many Korean people in their 20s can relate to them.

849
01:22:55,210 --> 01:23:01,110
Korea is a country with very intense competition.

850
01:23:01,110 --> 01:23:04,070
It's a country with fierce competition.

851
01:23:04,070 --> 01:23:10,310
So depending on your age, you constantly face big worries.

852
01:23:10,310 --> 01:23:17,070
In high school, there's the stress of entrance exams,

853
01:23:17,070 --> 01:23:21,430
and when you become a university student, you have to prepare for employment.

854
01:23:21,430 --> 01:23:29,300
Especially before and after graduating from university, there are many hardships.

855
01:23:29,300 --> 01:23:36,620
From now on, I want to talk about these realities of Korean culture.

856
01:23:36,620 --> 01:23:45,200
Korea has K-pop that people love, interesting dramas, and lots of delicious food.

857
01:23:45,200 --> 01:23:51,450
But behind that, there are also these realistic stories.

858
01:23:51,450 --> 01:23:53,550
Realistic stories.

859
01:23:53,550 --> 01:23:55,550
People's stories.

860
01:23:55,550 --> 01:24:01,750
I think reality is a little different in every country.

861
01:24:01,750 --> 01:24:06,450
Every country has different cultures and characteristics.

862
01:24:06,450 --> 01:24:20,850
But since I am Korean, I want to share my own stories and the stories of people around me that I have experienced in Korea.

863
01:24:20,850 --> 01:24:26,150
And I want to hear your stories too.

864
01:24:26,150 --> 01:24:34,950
Yes, today we started with the World Cup story, but suddenly it got a bit serious.

865
01:24:34,950 --> 01:24:39,590
But wasn't today's podcast fun?

866
01:24:39,590 --> 01:24:45,410
The World Cup is a game where many countries come together.

867
01:24:45,410 --> 01:24:53,700
So today, it was really fun to be able to talk about various countries.

868
01:24:53,700 --> 01:24:57,760
Which country are you from?

869
01:24:57,760 --> 01:25:04,680
Are there still World Cup matches left in your country?

870
01:25:04,680 --> 01:25:09,610
Then your country must be really good at soccer.

871
01:25:09,610 --> 01:25:15,000
Please tell me a lot about soccer in your country too.

872
01:25:15,000 --> 01:25:19,480
Ah, and there was a question I mentioned earlier.

873
01:25:19,480 --> 01:25:24,510
Who is your country's rival?

874
01:25:24,510 --> 01:25:31,960
Is there a country that always competes with you in sports games?

875
01:25:31,960 --> 01:25:37,880
Or you can also predict the winner of this World Cup.

876
01:25:37,880 --> 01:25:42,710
Which country do you think will win?

877
01:25:42,710 --> 01:25:48,530
My student said he thinks France will win.

878
01:25:48,530 --> 01:25:52,780
I'm really curious about your thoughts too.

879
01:25:52,780 --> 01:25:57,960
Anyway, it's July, so it's very hot, right?

880
01:25:57,960 --> 01:26:05,650
I hope you relieve your stress by watching the fun World Cup.

881
01:26:05,650 --> 01:26:12,810
Ah... after the World Cup ends, we have to wait another four years.

882
01:26:12,810 --> 01:26:16,630
The more I think about it, the more disappointing it is.

883
01:26:16,630 --> 01:26:20,990
I'm disappointed that Korea was eliminated so quickly.

884
01:26:20,990 --> 01:26:28,340
I don't think it was a game that should have ended so quickly, so it's really disappointing.

885
01:26:28,340 --> 01:26:38,010
Still, today I feel relieved because I was able to talk about soccer freely on this podcast.

886
01:26:38,010 --> 01:26:45,870
If I talk about soccer again, it will probably be in four years, right?

887
01:26:45,870 --> 01:26:51,700
Everyone, I really have one last thing I want to say.

888
01:26:51,700 --> 01:26:58,540
Recently, someone tagged me on Instagram.

889
01:26:58,540 --> 01:27:04,150
It was a Polish subscriber who listens to my podcast.

890
01:27:04,150 --> 01:27:11,130
They posted that they are studying Korean while listening to my podcast.

891
01:27:11,130 --> 01:27:18,470
And they introduced my podcast to other Polish people.

892
01:27:18,470 --> 01:27:20,650
Thank you so much.

893
01:27:20,650 --> 01:27:25,940
I also visited their Instagram.

894
01:27:25,940 --> 01:27:34,040
It seems they teach Polish people about Korean.

895
01:27:34,040 --> 01:27:43,320
Actually, I said I would use Instagram often in the past... but I hardly did.

896
01:27:43,320 --> 01:27:48,270
But seeing this person motivated me.

897
01:27:48,270 --> 01:27:49,860
A good motivation.

898
01:27:49,860 --> 01:27:59,620
Thank you sincerely for loving Korean and teaching Korean to Polish people like this.

899
01:27:59,620 --> 01:28:05,160
I will also upload more good content from now on.

900
01:28:05,160 --> 01:28:11,650
I will try to post my daily photos on Instagram too.

901
01:28:11,650 --> 01:28:16,980
If you're curious, come to my Instagram anytime, everyone!

902
01:28:16,980 --> 01:28:26,150
Anyway, I'm curious how much your Korean skills will have improved in four years.

903
01:28:26,150 --> 01:28:31,070
You will still be studying Korean then, right?

904
01:28:31,070 --> 01:28:36,210
Until then, let's study hard together.

905
01:28:36,210 --> 01:28:41,280
Let's have fun studying Korean together! Okay?

906
01:28:41,280 --> 01:28:55,000
Then, see you in the next podcast! Bye :)
